1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,225
Running a household is a rolling list of small
decisions that never stops.

2
00:00:04,225 --> 00:00:05,950
Have the kids outgrown their shoes?

3
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:09,025
Is a gift sorted for the anniversary in two
weeks?

4
00:00:09,025 --> 00:00:11,150
Did the grocery reorder go out?

5
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:17,975
None of these is hard, but doing them well means
remembering last year, checking today's prices,
and staying inside a budget.

6
00:00:17,975 --> 00:00:21,425
Household Chief of Staff is a background agent
that carries that list.

7
00:00:21,425 --> 00:00:29,975
Once a day it sweeps the family's data, checks
every idea against a budget, and emails you one
decision at a time: approve, or reject.

8
00:00:29,975 --> 00:00:32,350
The design decision that matters is trust.

9
00:00:32,350 --> 00:00:38,000
If an agent is going to email me spending
decisions, I need to know it can't talk itself
into one.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,675
So the model never decides whether to act, and
never decides whether something is affordable —
both are plain Python.

11
00:00:44,675 --> 00:00:47,600
Trigger rules run first; a budget guardrail runs
last.

12
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:54,900
The Strands agent in between has one job: given
a qualified candidate and the real data, write
the proposal and justify it.

13
00:00:54,900 --> 00:01:01,317
It runs on AWS — Strands Agents on Amazon
Bedrock with the Nova model, one agent per
category

14
00:01:01,317 --> 00:01:08,206
a daily EventBridge schedule firing the sweep
into Lambda, DynamoDB for the data, S E S for
the email

15
00:01:08,206 --> 00:01:11,650
and Bedrock AgentCore for the part you can talk
to.

16
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:13,625
This is the live stack in us-east-one.

17
00:01:13,625 --> 00:01:16,001
Here is a sweep running — one Lambda invoke.

18
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:19,451
It checked all four categories and created ten
proposals.

19
00:01:19,451 --> 00:01:20,501
Start with wardrobe.

20
00:01:20,501 --> 00:01:24,672
All three children are more than ninety days
overdue for a sizing review

21
00:01:24,672 --> 00:01:29,301
so each gets a proposal in their latest measured
sizes and their own preferences.

22
00:01:29,301 --> 00:01:32,697
Take Kai: his size history is a hundred and ten
days stale

23
00:01:32,697 --> 00:01:37,763
so the agent proposes red dinosaur-themed shoes
in E U twenty-seven with a matching top

24
00:01:37,763 --> 00:01:41,851
at fifty pounds — inside the hundred and twenty
pound wardrobe budget.

25
00:01:41,851 --> 00:01:42,601
Then gifts.

26
00:01:42,601 --> 00:01:48,026
Elena and Marcus's anniversary is eleven days
out, inside the fourteen-day window.

27
00:01:48,026 --> 00:01:51,939
The agent checked the gift history first: last
year was a spa voucher

28
00:01:51,939 --> 00:01:56,076
so this year it proposes something different — a
personalized photo book.

29
00:01:56,076 --> 00:02:03,126
At a hundred and twenty pounds it sits just over
the gifts budget, so it is flagged for an
override, not sent as a clean approve.

30
00:02:03,126 --> 00:02:04,151
Then groceries.

31
00:02:04,151 --> 00:02:09,608
Four staples are past their reorder cycle — milk
and bread a day over, coffee six days over

32
00:02:09,608 --> 00:02:16,239
toilet paper five — each reordered from its
usual store at its usual price, twenty-seven
pounds twenty in total

33
00:02:16,239 --> 00:02:18,176
well inside the groceries budget.

34
00:02:18,176 --> 00:02:18,876
And travel.

35
00:02:18,876 --> 00:02:26,076
Round-trip flights for five to Zurich came in at
thirteen hundred and sixty-five pounds, to Bali
at twenty-four hundred and sixty-five.

36
00:02:26,076 --> 00:02:32,397
Both beat their target price, but both are far
over the five hundred pound travel budget, so
they are flagged too — surfaced

37
00:02:32,397 --> 00:02:33,551
never silently dropped.

38
00:02:33,551 --> 00:02:35,232
Here is Kai's justification

39
00:02:35,232 --> 00:02:42,137
shown on the proposal itself: he grew from shoe
E U twenty-five to twenty-seven over the last
hundred and ten days

40
00:02:42,137 --> 00:02:47,601
and the recommendation follows his latest
measurement of a hundred and sixteen
centimetres.

41
00:02:47,601 --> 00:02:55,476
Every number there comes from the real DynamoDB
record — the actual size history, the actual
gift history — not a template.

42
00:02:55,476 --> 00:03:01,876
Every proposal is one email — category,
proposal, amount, reason, and two links: approve
or reject.

43
00:03:01,876 --> 00:03:04,401
Open Kai's wardrobe email and click approve.

44
00:03:04,401 --> 00:03:11,909
That hits API Gateway and a Lambda, which checks
a per-transaction token, flips the transaction
to executed in DynamoDB

45
00:03:11,909 --> 00:03:16,226
and moves the wardrobe budget counter by exactly
the approved amount.

46
00:03:16,226 --> 00:03:19,851
Nothing was bought — this is a simulated
execution model.

47
00:03:19,851 --> 00:03:22,301
Now reject the photo book instead — one click.

48
00:03:22,301 --> 00:03:25,426
It flips to rejected, and the gifts budget stays
untouched.

49
00:03:25,426 --> 00:03:32,001
Every proposal cleared a deterministic budget
check before it was ever emailed — plain Python,
not the model.

50
00:03:32,001 --> 00:03:34,451
The model proposes; the code enforces.

51
00:03:34,451 --> 00:03:42,175
And this is the whole system on one page: the
approval queue by category, today's activity,
what is trending toward qualifying next

52
00:03:42,175 --> 00:03:50,426
and budget bars where the shaded overlay is what
is pending — so you can see whether approving
everything queued would push a category over.

53
00:03:50,426 --> 00:03:51,201
One more thing.

54
00:03:51,201 --> 00:03:57,876
The interactive path is a Strands agents-as-
tools orchestrator on Bedrock AgentCore, so I
can just ask it things.

55
00:03:57,876 --> 00:04:01,526
Summarise today's proposals, and flag anything
over budget.

56
00:04:01,526 --> 00:04:06,684
It consults the proposal log and the live
budgets, and answers in a couple of sentences —
here

57
00:04:06,684 --> 00:04:11,251
calling out travel at thirty-eight hundred
pounds against a five hundred pound limit.

58
00:04:11,251 --> 00:04:14,226
AgentCore Memory keeps that context across runs.

59
00:04:14,226 --> 00:04:20,376
That is the loop: deterministic rules on the
edges, a Strands agent in the middle, one
decision at a time.

60
00:04:20,376 --> 00:04:27,101
It is all on GitHub — Apache-2.0, with a test
suite that runs with no AWS access.

61
00:04:27,101 --> 00:04:28,401
Household Chief of Staff.

62
00:04:28,401 --> 00:04:31,627
It watches, it decides, it justifies — and it
asks first.

